WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:16.640
<v Ira Glass>Not long ago, the guy who headed the youth group at Covenant Presbyterian Church decided to hold a debate. Half the kids in the youth group had to argue for Christianity. Half the kids had to make the case that Christianity was all made up, a fairy tale. And the Christians lost.
<v Ira Glass>不久前，圣约长老会青年团契的负责人决定举办一场辩论。青年团契里一半的孩子必须为基督教辩护，另一半孩子则要论证基督教全是虚构的，只是一个童话故事。结果，基督教派输了。

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.790
<v Dan Adamson>And I think the number one reason why is because they tried to just come at it with a lot of facts.
<v Dan Adamson>我认为最主要的原因是，他们试图仅凭大量事实来赢得辩论。

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.040
<v Ira Glass>Dan Adamson is the youth leader.
<v Ira Glass>丹·亚当森是青年团契的领袖。

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:31.310
<v Dan Adamson>And it was amazing to watch these-- the ones that were debating Christianity said, Jesus is real. There's people that found him, and there's proof on this, and they-- evidence.
<v Dan Adamson>看着这些孩子真是令人惊叹——那些为基督教辩护的孩子说，耶稣是真实存在的。有人找到了他，这有证据，他们有证据。

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.390
<v Ira Glass>Archaeological records.
<v Ira Glass>考古记录。

00:00:32.390 --> 00:01:15.210
<v Dan Adamson>Archaeological records. And what was so awesome, by the non-Christian debate team, is they said, fine. We totally acknowledge that Jesus is real. But so was Mohammed. So was Buddha. So was this. And the Christians were like, yeah, but Jesus is real. And he died on the cross and he rose again. And the non-Christians came back and said, well, there's people that believe that. But there's also people that believe Mohammed was this. And what I wish the Christians would have done, would have bee said, here am I. I believe God is real because He's changed my life. And put myself on the witness stand and say, here's what I was before God and here's who I am now.
<v Dan Adamson>考古记录。非基督教辩论队最棒的地方在于，他们说，好吧，我们完全承认耶稣是真实存在的。但穆罕默德也是，佛陀也是，这些人都是。而基督徒们则说，是的，但耶稣是真实的，他死在十字架上，然后复活了。非基督徒反驳道，确实有人相信这一点，但也有人相信穆罕默德是这样的。我希望基督徒当时能做的是站出来说，我在这里，我相信上帝是真实的，因为他改变了我的生命。将自己放在见证人的位置上，说出我在上帝面前过去是什么样，现在又是什么样。

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:33.190
<v Ira Glass>The problem is, for the teenagers in this youth group, the phrase, here's who I was before I found God, it has no meaning. They all come from families that discuss the Bible at dinner. They go to church every Sunday. They come from the kind of homes where all the kids can tell you how many swears are in the movie Titanic, because every TV show and film is pre-screened.
<v Ira Glass>问题在于，对于这个青年团契的青少年来说，“我在找到上帝之前是什么样”这句话毫无意义。他们都来自在晚餐时讨论圣经的家庭。他们每周日都去教堂。他们来自那种所有孩子都能告诉你电影《泰坦尼克号》里有多少句脏话的家庭，因为所有的电视节目和电影都是经过预先筛选的。

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:48.280
<v Dan Adamson>I have kids that are willing to serve, kids that know the Bible, kids that know their theology. This is the only truth they've known. They've been told from the very beginning God loves them, God has a plan for them. Jesus is their Savior. They've been singing "Jesus Loves Me" since they've been five years old.
<v Dan Adamson>我有一些乐于奉献的孩子，他们了解圣经，了解神学。这是他们所知的唯一真理。他们从一开始就被告知上帝爱他们，上帝为他们有计划，耶稣是他们的救主。他们五岁起就在唱《耶稣爱我》。

00:01:48.280 --> 00:02:24.640
<v Ira Glass>It's time for them to develop their own faith, not just to parrot back what their parents say. Dan says this. Most of the parents say it. They need to question what they've learned and come to their own conclusions. If they don't do it now, Dan says, they'll do it later, without parents and a youth pastor to nudge them toward God. So Dan decided to take them to West Virginia. It will be a mission trip, the youth group's first mission trip. They'd have to help poor people. They'd have to testify about God's word to strangers. It would be hard, and hardship can make you turn to God. And they'd see the kind of things that make people engage serious questions about their faith.
<v Ira Glass>现在是时候让他们发展出自己的信仰，而不是仅仅鹦鹉学舌般地重复父母的话。丹这么说，大多数父母也这么说。他们需要质疑自己所学的东西，并得出自己的结论。丹说，如果他们现在不这么做，以后也会做，到时候就没有父母和青年牧师引导他们走向上帝了。于是丹决定带他们去西弗吉尼亚州。这将是一次宣教之旅，是青年团契的第一次宣教之旅。他们将不得不帮助穷人，不得不向陌生人见证上帝的话语。这会很艰难，而苦难能让人转向上帝。他们会看到那些促使人们深入思考信仰的问题。

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:46.610
<v Dan Adamson>If they begin doubting because they look in this world and they see hard lives in West Virginia, they see people that are hurting, and they see this and they look up and they think, why would God allow that to happen? That's like an example of I think where I'd start seeing more growth.
<v Dan Adamson>如果他们开始怀疑，因为他们观察这个世界，看到了西弗吉尼亚州艰难的生活，看到了受伤的人，他们看着上面想，为什么上帝允许这种事发生？我想这就是我开始看到更多成长的地方。

00:02:46.610 --> 00:03:02.580
<v Ira Glass>So that was the plan. Now, let's meet some of the teenagers. Kelly Hoover's age 14, long brown hair, braces, lip gloss. Kelly's outgoing and bright and she's somebody capable of saying exactly what is on her mind.
<v Ira Glass>这就是计划。现在，来认识一下这些青少年吧。凯莉·胡佛14岁，留着长长的棕色头发，戴着牙套，涂着唇彩。凯莉性格开朗、聪明，是一个能准确表达自己想法的人。

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:32.340
<v Kelly Hoover>Well, I really want to meet the kids there and just-- because I mean, I don't want to sound snotty or anything but-- I'm more privileged than most people. I mean, I live in a big house. I have a pool. And I don't really see kids a lot who don't have as much as I do. And I really just want to see what it's like, and how living with them is. And I really just want to teach them about Jesus, because I don't know how I could live without Him. And sometimes I think how blessed I was to be born in such a family.
<v Kelly Hoover>嗯，我真的很想去见那里的孩子们，因为——我的意思是，我不想听起来很势利或者怎么样——但我比大多数人幸运。我的意思是，我住在大房子里，我有游泳池。我不太常看到像我这样拥有不多东西的孩子。我真的很想看看那是什么样子，和他们住在一起是什么感觉。我真的只想教他们认识耶稣，因为我不知道没有他我该怎么活。有时候我想，出生在这样的家庭是多么有福。

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:51.310
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, A Teenager's Guide to God, the story of five adults, 11 kids, and six days in West Virginia. Of adults trying to mold the faith of teenagers and things not working out quite as planned.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目是《青少年上帝指南》，讲述了5名成年人、11名孩子在西弗吉尼亚州六天的故事。讲述成年人试图塑造青少年的信仰，以及事情并没有完全按计划进行的过程。

00:03:51.310 --> 00:04:14.730
<v Ira Glass>It's an odd fact of religious life in America that in this country founded by Christians, in which a majority of people say they believe in God and identify themselves as Christians, that so many religious Christians feel that they are an oppressed minority. They say the media doesn't share their values, that secular institutions undermine their beliefs. And the job of raising Christian children, they'll tell you, is like trying to do God's work from behind enemy lines.
<v Ira Glass>在美国宗教生活中，有一个奇怪的事实：在这个由基督徒建立、大多数人声称相信上帝并自认为是基督徒的国家里，许多虔诚的基督徒却感到自己是被压迫的少数群体。他们说媒体不认同他们的价值观，世俗机构破坏了他们的信仰。他们会告诉你，抚养基督徒子女的工作就像是在敌后开展上帝的工作。

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:44.980
<v Ira Glass>Today we are devoting our entire program to the kids that they're struggling to bring up. This American Life producer Susan Burton and I spent a week this August recording these teenagers, day and night, throughout their mission trip. And we bring you this portrait of what it means to be a Christian teenager in America, for some kids anyway, around the turn of the millennium. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们整个节目都献给他们努力抚养的这些孩子。《美国生活》制片人苏珊·伯顿和我今年八月花了一周时间，昼夜不停地记录了这些青少年在宣教之旅中的表现。我们为您呈现这些千禧年之交的基督徒青少年（至少是部分孩子）的生活画像。请继续收听。

00:04:44.980 --> 00:05:10.960
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Exodus. The youth group is part of Covenant Presbyterian Church here in Chicago, a city church with lots of members who live out in the suburbs. A number of their parents were founding members of the church, less than two decades ago. These teenagers are 13 to 17. They all grew up attending Christian schools or homeschools. Our trip began with a 14-hour drive in two rented vans to West Virginia.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，出埃及。这个青年团契隶属于芝加哥的圣约长老会教堂，这是一座有许多成员居住在郊区的城市教堂。他们的许多父母不到二十年前就是教堂的创始成员。这些青少年年龄在13到17岁之间。他们都在基督教学校或在家接受教育中长大。我们的旅程始于乘坐两辆租来的面包车前往西弗吉尼亚州，车程14小时。

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:18.630
<v Girl 1>Can we hear LFO? Because Ira has never heard it. Ira, have you heard the summer girls song? Summertime girls? Oh, it's so funny. Where's my bag?
<v Girl 1>我们可以听LFO的歌吗？因为艾拉从来没听过。艾拉，你听过那首夏日女孩歌吗？夏日女孩？哦，太有趣了。我的包在哪？

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:40.050
<v Ira Glass>We were barely in the van 10 minutes when Marisa and Kelly asked Dan to play the song that became the unofficial theme song to our trip. It wasn't by Jars of Clay or dc Talk or any of the other Christian pop bands whose CDs we'd brought. It was a rap song, by a white, one-hit wonder boy band called LFO. Everybody spent the ride down trying to memorize the lyrics.
<v Ira Glass>我们上车还没十分钟，玛丽莎和凯莉就要求丹播放那首成为我们旅程非官方主题曲的歌。那不是Jars of Clay或dc Talk，也不是我们带的任何其他基督教流行乐队的作品。这是一首说唱歌曲，由一个只有一首热门单曲的白人男子组合LFO演唱。大家在旅途中都在努力背诵歌词。

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:40.450
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SUMMER GIRLS" BY LFO]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - LFO的《SUMMER GIRLS》]

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:54.930
<v Girl 1>Ah! I missed it! I missed the Billy Shakespeare-- that's the-- I wait. Sonnets! OK, OK, OK -- start it over. I  got it. So it's after the Larry Bird. What does he say?
<v Girl 1>啊！我错过了！我错过了比利·莎士比亚——那是——等一下。十四行诗！好了，好了，好了——重新开始。我记住了。是在拉里·伯德之后。他说什么？

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:24.570
<v Ira Glass>Dan is 29, married, with a new baby. But in the van, he's more like a counselor adored by his campers than youth pastor. The girls strain their necks and try to meet his eyes in the rear-view mirror as they talk, asking what he and one of the other leaders, Dave, were like back in college together at the evocatively named Moody Bible Institute. Teasing him about the songs he likes, which, as far as they're concerned, are impossibly old-- 10, maybe 15 years. He tries just as hard as anybody to learn the words to "Summer Girls."
<v Ira Glass>丹29岁，已婚，有一个新生儿。但在车里，他更像是一个被露营者崇拜的辅导员，而不是青年牧师。女孩们伸长脖子，在交谈时试图在后视镜里与他目光接触，问他和另一位领袖戴夫在那些名字引人注目的穆迪圣经学院读大学时是什么样子。取笑他喜欢的歌曲，对她们来说，那些歌已经老得不可思议了——大概有10年、15年了。他像其他人一样努力学习《Summer Girls》的歌词。

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.000
<v Dan Adamson>[SINGING] Macaulay Culkin was home alone.
<v Dan Adamson>[演唱] 麦考利·卡尔金独自在家。

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.460
<v Dan Adamson>[MUSIC - "SUMMER GIRLS" BY LFO]
<v Dan Adamson>[音乐 - LFO的《SUMMER GIRLS》]

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.930
<v Dan Adamson>[SINGING] --was Alex P. Keaton.
<v Dan Adamson>[演唱] ——是亚历克斯·P·基顿。

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:33.930
<v Girl 1>What is that from?
<v Girl 1>那出自哪里？

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.820
<v Girl 2>You know, Family Ties?
<v Girl 2>你知道，《成长的烦恼》？

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:37.580
<v Girl 1>Oh, I never watched that show.
<v Girl 1>哦，我从没看过那个节目。

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.860
<v Girl 2>I did. Sometimes. And Mallory, and what was that other girl's name?
<v Girl 2>我看过。有时。还有马洛里，那个女孩叫什么名字？

00:06:41.860 --> 00:07:06.460
<v Ira Glass>That's too '80s for me, Arden says, I couldn't watch it. These girls are the children that the Christian right has in mind when it holds press conferences on what's at stake in America's culture war. They are cannon fodder in that war. Just a few years before they were born, a child psychologist named Dr. James Dobson founded a group called Focus on the Family. It is an enormous religious organization based in Colorado Springs.
<v Ira Glass>“那对我来说太八十年代了，”阿登说，“我看不下去。”这些女孩正是基督教右翼在就美国文化战争利害关系举行新闻发布会时所考虑的对象。她们是那场战争中的炮灰。在她们出生前几年，一位名叫詹姆斯·多布森博士的儿童心理学家创立了一个名为“爱家协会”的组织。这是一个总部位于科罗拉多斯普林斯的庞大宗教组织。

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:42.640
<v Ira Glass>Dobson's genius is that he offers exhaustive, concrete advice on everything from child discipline to movie-going to a generation of parents who are trying to raise their kids in a new way, or rather, in a very old way. As part of that, Dobson has built an entire superstructure to shield young people from Hollywood, from rap music, from the WB. Focus on the Family publishes an ongoing encyclopedic guide which evaluates pop culture from a Christian perspective for parents. In addition, they've created an empire of Christian pop of their own-- original radio dramas and children's videos, books, websites, and several magazines.
<v Ira Glass>多布森的天才之处在于，他为那些试图以新方式——或者说，以非常古老的方式——抚养孩子的父母们提供了从儿童纪律到观看电影等各方面的详尽、具体的建议。作为其中的一部分，多布森建立了一整套上层建筑，旨在保护年轻人免受好莱坞、说唱音乐和WB电视台的影响。“爱家协会”出版了一份持续更新的百科全书式指南，从基督徒的角度为父母评估流行文化。此外，他们还建立了一个属于自己的基督教流行文化帝国——原创广播剧和儿童视频、书籍、网站以及几本杂志。

00:07:42.640 --> 00:08:07.540
<v Ira Glass>It is a remarkably effective social experiment. These teenagers don't even fight with their parents about what they can watch and listen to. At this point, they generally agree that certain films and shows are bad for them. And if you ask them about any of the TV shows targeted at and beloved by so many of their 14- and 15-year-old peers-- Dawson's, Buffy, Felicity-- you can hear their parents' voices and Dr. Dobson's voice in their replies.
<v Ira Glass>这是一项非常有效的社会实验。这些青少年甚至不会因为能看什么、听什么而与父母争吵。在这一点上，他们通常认为某些电影和节目对他们有害。如果你问他们关于许多14、15岁同龄人喜爱并追捧的电视节目（如《道森的青春》、《吸血鬼猎人巴菲》、《费莉希蒂》）的看法，你会从他们的回答中听到父母和多布森博士的声音。

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:12.950
<v Girl 2>You feel really guilty walking away, that you just totally perverted your mind with all this stuff.
<v Girl 2>你走开时会感到非常内疚，觉得你完全用这些东西扭曲了自己的思想。

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:38.440
<v Girl 1>It's so unrealistic and sick. The fact that a boy is sleeping with his teacher, and he's like 15 years old. I mean, and these people are supposed to be 15 and 16 years old. And if that was how my life was, I would be dead right now. Because that's just ridiculous. Everybody is jumping in bed with everyone else and is all switching. I mean, by the middle of the series, I was hearing through rumors that so-and-so, who had been with the teacher, is now with this girl, who was actually on depressants. I mean, I have no idea what's going on.
<v Girl 1>这太不现实而且病态了。一个男孩竟然和他的老师睡觉，他才15岁。我的意思是，这些人应该才15、16岁。如果我的生活是那样，我现在肯定已经死了。因为那太荒谬了。每个人都和别人上床，又不停地交换。我的意思是，在剧集进行到一半时，我听到传言说，和老师在一起的那个人现在又和那个吃抗抑郁药的女孩在一起。我完全不知道在发生什么。

00:08:38.440 --> 00:09:04.060
<v Ira Glass>It's Andie, one of the supposed non-viewers pipes up from the back. What's so interesting about these girls is the odd mix of Christian and secular pop in their lives. Despite their Christian schools, despite their carefully monitored TV viewing, they do not seem out of touch. They're not un-hip. And here is the most important thing that they would want you to know about them. They like being Christians. This is Lauren.
<v Ira Glass>“是安迪，”其中一个所谓的“非观众”在后面插嘴说。这些女孩最有趣的地方在于，她们生活中基督教和世俗流行文化的奇怪结合。尽管有基督教学校，尽管有严加监视的电视观看习惯，她们看起来并不与世隔绝。她们并不落伍。而这是她们最想让你了解的一点：她们喜欢成为基督徒。这是劳伦。

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:16.540
<v Lauren>It's hard for people to understand that we really enjoy this religion. That us, as teenagers, who are the ones who are out to have fun and whatever, that this is actually something that means something to us, and that we are having fun doing this.
<v Lauren>人们很难理解我们真的很享受这种宗教。我们作为青少年，那些追求乐趣的人，这实际上对我们意义重大，而且我们乐在其中。

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:47.940
<v Ira Glass>As they've gotten older, some of them have taken more steps, carefully measured steps, into the secular world. Ruth and Jessica and Anna go to a public high school, and it isn't that kids at the school are hostile to Christianity, as they were, Dan says, when he was a teenager. These days, the attitude at the school is, if Christianity is your thing, that's fine, just so long as you don't push it onto other people. Of all the teenagers in the youth group, the one struggling the most with how to live as a religious person in the secular world is one of the two boys on the trip, Joel.
<v Ira Glass>随着年龄的增长，她们中的一些人已经谨慎地迈出了走向世俗世界的步伐。露丝、杰西卡和安娜在公立高中上学，这并不是说学校里的孩子像丹年轻时那样对基督教有敌意。现在，学校里的态度是：如果基督教是你的兴趣所在，那没问题，只要你不把它强加给别人就行。在青年团契的所有青少年中，在世俗世界中如何以宗教人士身份生活而感到最挣扎的人，是这次旅行中仅有的两名男孩之一——乔尔。

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:55.140
<v Joel>I've had my share of dealings with kids who've been at public school, and I just don't really like what I see.
<v Joel>我处理过不少公立学校孩子的事，我真的不喜欢我所看到的。

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:17.450
<v Ira Glass>Joel's 13 and probably the most sheltered person in the group. His mother's always homeschooled him because, he says, she feels that Christian schools only teach Christian values during Bible class. She feels it should be enunciated in all the subjects. Joel's getting out more and more. Just this summer, he spent three weeks away from home for the first time in his life. And it was with non-Christian kids on a soccer trip to England.
<v Ira Glass>乔尔13岁，可能是团契里最受保护的孩子。他母亲一直让他接受在家教育，因为他说，她觉得基督教学校只在圣经课上教基督教价值观。她认为应该在所有科目中都体现出来。乔尔出门的机会越来越多。就在今年夏天，他第一次离家三周，是和非基督徒孩子一起去英格兰进行足球旅行。

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:48.940
<v Joel>I don't know. I mean, I learned a lot of stories and jokes and stuff. But they were all really nasty and unrepeatable, if that gives you an idea of, you know, what kids learn in public schools. And it's like, the boys are always talking about sex or things that have to do with sex. And you know, I mean, at this age, yeah, I'm starting to think about that, but still I don't like talking about it.
<v Joel>我不知道。我的意思是，我学了很多故事、笑话之类的东西。但它们都非常肮脏且不堪入耳，如果你能想象公立学校的孩子学到了什么的话。就像，男孩们总是谈论性或者与性有关的事情。你知道，我的意思是，在这个年纪，是的，我开始思考这个问题，但我还是不喜欢谈论它。

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:59.960
<v Ira Glass>A typical joke? The only one that I could drag out of him involved a boy, a girl having her period, a car late at night, a policeman, and a pizza. Believe me, you don't want to know.
<v Ira Glass>典型的笑话？我唯一能从他那里问出来的涉及一个男孩、一个生理期的女孩、一辆深夜的车、一个警察和一个披萨。相信我，你不会想知道的。

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:10.720
<v Joel>It was just incredible. I mean, they were-- it's just, they were the most-- and they didn't-- but--
<v Joel>真是太不可思议了。我的意思是，他们是——只是，他们是……而且他们没有——但是——

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:13.030
<v Ira Glass>He is literally at a loss for words.
<v Ira Glass>他真的到了无话可说的地步。

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:44.020
<v Joel>And then some of the guys just got a kick out of acting like they were gay or something. On the bus ride they found out that that annoyed me, so they did that the rest of the trip. Acting like they were after me, if you know what I mean. They would say stuff in kind of an exaggerated, effeminate kind of accent. There was one guy who would always wink at me. And I'm like, Matt, knock it off. And he's like, oh, come on. You know you want it. And I'm like, no, I don't.
<v Joel>然后一些家伙竟然以此为乐，假装自己是同性恋什么的。在公交车上，他们发现这让我很烦，于是他们就在旅程剩下的时间里一直那样做。假装他们在追求我，如果你明白我的意思。他们会用一种夸张的、女性化的口音说些话。有个家伙总是对我眨眼。我说，马特，停下来。他说，哦，得了吧，你知道你想要的。我说，不，我才不想要。

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:54.530
<v Ira Glass>Up until this point, you've been homeschooled. You haven't been around other kids. And so, was this literally the first time this had happened to you?
<v Ira Glass>到目前为止，你一直接受在家教育。你没怎么接触过其他孩子。那么，这真的是第一次发生这种事吗？

00:11:54.530 --> 00:12:03.230
<v Joel>Yes.
<v Joel>是的。

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:20.720
<v Ira Glass>Compared with all the other kids on the trip, Joel's family is much more fundamentalist in the way that it incorporates the Bible into their daily lives. Whatever benefits he gets from that, the kids whose parents try to balance secular and Christian influences all seem way more at ease in themselves and in the world.
<v Ira Glass>与其他参加旅行的孩子相比，乔尔的家庭在将圣经融入日常生活方面要原教旨主义得多。无论他从中得到什么好处，那些试图平衡世俗和基督教影响的父母的孩子，看起来在自我和世界中要自在得多。

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:25.370
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "SUMMER GIRLS" BY LFO]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - LFO的《SUMMER GIRLS》]

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:29.930
<v Multiple Girls And Boys>[SINGING] I like girls that wear Abercrombie and Fitch. I'd take her if I had one wish.
<v Multiple Girls And Boys>[演唱] 我喜欢穿Abercrombie and Fitch的女孩。如果有一个愿望，我会带走她。

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:47.400
<v Ira Glass>For the rest of the drive, the girls sing the LFO song every few hours. They ruffle through the pages of Teen People , read stories out loud to each other, close the magazine in their laps, flip it over and begin again. By the time we arrive in West Virginia, this is the first accomplishment of the trip. Nearly everybody has memorized all the words.
<v Ira Glass>在剩余的旅途中，女孩们每隔几个小时就会唱一次LFO的那首歌。她们翻阅《Teen People》杂志，大声读故事给彼此听，把杂志合上放在腿上，翻过来，然后再开始。当我们到达西弗吉尼亚州时，这是旅程的第一个成就：几乎每个人都背下了所有的歌词。

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:56.010
<v Multiple Girls And Boys>[SINGING] New Kids on the Block had a bunch of hits. Chinese food makes me sick. And I think it's fly when girls stop by for the summer, for the summer.
<v Multiple Girls And Boys>[演唱] “新街边男孩”有很多热门曲目。中国菜让我恶心。当女孩们在夏天顺道拜访时，我觉得很酷，在这个夏天，在这个夏天。

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:59.640
<v Ira Glass>These are the vessels that God has chosen to come here to help others.
<v Ira Glass>这些就是上帝选择来到这里帮助他人的容器。

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:07.700
<v Multiple Girls And Boys>[SINGING] I'll take her if I had one wish. But she's been gone since that summer, since that summer.
<v Multiple Girls And Boys>[演唱] 如果有一个愿望，我会带走她。但她从那个夏天起就离开了，从那个夏天起。

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:32.550
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Unto the Uttermost Part of the Earth. Everybody stays at an old 4-H camp that had been taken over by the Presbyterian Church of America's national missionary organization, Mission to the World. Kids and adults from all over the country filled the various cabins. Prayers were held in the campfire circle. It was green, shady, spectacularly pretty, with daily sermons from a tattooed ex-Marine everybody called Pastor Kenny.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，传到地极。大家住在一个旧的4-H营地，这个营地已被美国长老会全国宣教组织“使命世界”接管。来自全国各地的孩子和成年人挤满了各个小屋。在营火圈举行祷告。那里绿树成荫，风景极其优美，每个人都称之为“肯尼牧师”的纹身前海军陆战队员每天都会进行布道。

00:13:32.550 --> 00:14:11.560
<v Pastor Kenny>We've had people come up here for-- this is our sixth summer. And every year, someone comes with the idea that we're going to win West Virginia over to Jesus this week. I'm serious. Now this is true. I'm here to tell you that's not going to happen. It's not going to happen. But if you will open your heart to God this week, God will change your life, and you will never be the same.
<v Pastor Kenny>我们已经在这儿待了六年了——这是我们第六个夏天。每年都有人带着“我们要在这周赢得西弗吉尼亚州归向耶稣”的想法来到这里。我是认真的，这是真的。我现在要告诉你们，这不会发生。这不会发生。但如果你这周向神敞开心扉，神会改变你的生命，你将永远不再是原来的你。

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:36.880
<v Ira Glass>Right now there's a boom in church projects like this one, that send people on one-to-two-week mission trips. This boom crosses all denominations. There are no great national estimates, but in this one Presbyterian denomination, not the biggest one, the number of short-term missionaries has more than doubled in just three years. Now it's over 5,000 souls. 750 of them came through this West Virginia summer camp this year, and the number's still climbing.
<v Ira Glass>现在，像这样的教会项目正处于繁荣期，它们派遣人们进行为期一到两周的宣教之旅。这种繁荣跨越了所有宗派。虽然没有确切的全国性估计，但仅在这个长老会派别（还不是最大的一个）中，短期宣教士的数量在短短三年内就翻了一番多。现在已经超过了5000人。今年有750人通过这个西弗吉尼亚州的夏令营来到这里，而且人数还在不断增加。

00:14:36.880 --> 00:15:21.300
<v Ira Glass>And so, among certain groups of Christian teenagers, going on a mission trip has become a rite of passage. There are upbeat articles and ads in the Christian teen magazines. One of the Chicago girls, Anna, says that of the 11 girls in her class at her old school, a small Christian junior high, all 11 went on mission trips this summer. And when they got to where they were going, they often got a jolt of culture shock. Our group found itself in a small town fixing up a house that had a beautiful sloping yard, apple trees, two porches. Sure, there were trailer homes up the street, but this house was, all and all, not what the kids thought of when they signed up to help the unfortunate. One girl said that she had envisioned something more like Cabrini-Green, the high-rise Chicago public housing project. Kelly, the one whose family has a pool, put it this way.
<v Ira Glass>因此，在某些基督徒青少年群体中，参加宣教之旅已成为一种成年礼。基督教青少年杂志上有欢快的文章和广告。芝加哥女孩之一的安娜说，她以前就读的一所小型基督教初中，班里的11个女孩今年夏天全部参加了宣教之旅。当她们到达目的地时，常常会受到文化冲击。我们的小组发现自己在一个小镇上，正在修缮一栋有着美丽的倾斜院子、苹果树和两个门廊的房子。当然，街上有活动房屋，但这栋房子总的来说并不是孩子们报名帮助不幸者时想到的样子。一个女孩说她预想的情况更像是芝加哥卡布里尼-格林那样的公共住宅高层项目。家里有游泳池的凯莉是这样说的：

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:33.120
<v Kelly Hoover>I didn't picture-- I mean, this house is a lot bigger than I thought it'd be. I thought it'd be like a rundown little shack. I mean, I didn't picture like, rat clothes, you know?
<v Kelly Hoover>我没想过——我的意思是，这房子比我想象的大得多。我以为会像个破烂的小棚屋。我的意思是我没想过是那种穷困潦倒的，你知道吗？

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.800
<v Ira Glass>Rat clothes, did you say?
<v Ira Glass>你说穷困潦倒？

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:43.930
<v Kelly Hoover>I don't know. Like, I don't know, like dirt poor. I mean, but it's nice. I mean, it definitely needs some painting work, but you know.
<v Kelly Hoover>我不知道。就是，我不知道，就像一贫如洗。但它很不错。我是说，它绝对需要粉刷一下，但你知道。

00:15:43.930 --> 00:16:02.550
<v Ira Glass>You might think it would be awkward for the recipients of this charity work. If nothing else, to have all the neighbors see a team of out-of-state church kids working on their house. But as it turned out, the neighbors saw the kids and thought that perhaps they were from some kind of work-release boot camp kind of program, like they saw on NBC Dateline, they told us.
<v Ira Glass>你可能认为接受这种慈善工作会很尴尬。至少，让所有的邻居看到一群外州的教会孩子在修他们的房子会很奇怪。但结果证明，邻居们看到这些孩子时，觉得他们可能是某种劳动教养项目的人，就像他们在NBC《Dateline》节目里看到的那样，他们告诉我们。

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:08.920
<v Pebble Cunningham>My dad-- not my family, my husband's family-- all worked in a coal mine.
<v Pebble Cunningham>我父亲——不是我的家人，是我丈夫的家人——都在煤矿工作过。

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:18.150
<v Ira Glass>Pebble Cunningham is 80 years old. She lives in the house that the Chicago group was painting, along with her daughter, who is in her 50s, and her granddaughter, Angie.
<v Ira Glass>佩布尔·坎宁安今年80岁。她和她50多岁的女儿以及孙女安吉住在这栋芝加哥小组正在粉刷的房子里。

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:25.720
<v Ira Glass>I would expect, for somebody like you, whose family's always worked, is it strange to have these people come in and help you out like this?
<v Ira Glass>对于像您这样家里一直都在工作的人来说，有人进来这样帮助你们，会不会觉得奇怪？

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:38.050
<v Pebble Cunningham>It's strange, but it's nice. Because we have no man-power at all. We ain't got no men to help us. This house was going down fast.
<v Pebble Cunningham>奇怪，但很好。因为我们根本没有劳动力。我们没有男人来帮我们。这房子正在迅速倒塌。

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:43.680
<v Ira Glass>But I would think that because you've always been a working family, the notion of having people come in and help out and do charity--
<v Ira Glass>但我以为因为你们一直是一个勤劳的家庭，对于有人进来帮助并进行慈善——

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:53.140
<v Pebble Cunningham>First time. But we loved it. Because we needed it, you know.
<v Pebble Cunningham>第一次。但我们很喜欢。因为我们需要它，你知道。

00:16:53.140 --> 00:16:59.720
<v Ira Glass>Now, were you worried, having all these Bible kids come to you, that they'll be trying to preach to you the whole time?
<v Ira Glass>那你们会担心吗？这些圣经孩子来找你们，他们会一直试图向你们传教？

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:10.770
<v Pebble Cunningham>Nah, let 'em talk about it. I [UNINTELLIGIBLE] to look forward to Jesus. [UNINTELLIGIBLE] got to look forward to.
<v Pebble Cunningham>不，让他们说吧。我期待着耶稣。有值得期待的事。

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:26.190
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Wise as Serpents and Harmless as Doves. Back in Chicago, Dan had said that part of what would bring everybody on the trip closer to God was facing hardship together. And, as if on schedule, the difficulties began our first morning.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，灵巧像蛇，驯良像鸽子。在芝加哥时，丹曾说，让大家在旅途中更亲近上帝的部分原因是共同面对苦难。正如预料的那样，困难在我们第一天早上就开始了。

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:30.040
<v Girl 1>Give me yellow hair. No, give me-- give me blue hair.
<v Girl 1>给我弄个黄头发。不，给我——给我弄个蓝头发。

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:33.710
<v Girl 2>You've got to give me like-- you've got to give me Scully hair.
<v Girl 2>你得给我弄个像斯卡莉那样的发型。

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:41.940
<v Ira Glass>The girls sit on the porch of their cabin, bent in concentration with crayons and paper, drawing a get-well card for Dan, with everybody on it. They also gave him Gatorade.
<v Ira Glass>女孩们坐在小屋的门廊上，专注地用蜡笔和纸画一张康复卡给丹，上面画着每个人。她们还给了他佳得乐。

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:44.220
<v Girl 3>Dan's probably going to have to go to the hospital.
<v Girl 3>丹可能得去医院了。

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:49.390
<v Girl 4>Yeah, he's really sick. He's like vomiting, barfing, vomiting, violently.
<v Girl 4>是的，他病得很厉害。他呕吐、干呕，呕吐得很厉害。

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:50.810
<v Girl 3>Maybe the Gatorade will just do a--
<v Girl 3>也许佳得乐会起作用——

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:54.560
<v Girl 5>Yeah, maybe the Gatorade will work miracles.
<v Girl 5>是的，也许佳得乐会创造奇迹。

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:58.760
<v Girl 4>Miracles can happen. That's like in our devotion.
<v Girl 4>奇迹会发生的。就像我们的灵修课里说的那样。

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:30.050
<v Ira Glass>Dan has some sort of food poisoning. When he goes into the hospital, it sends the four adult leaders on the trip lurching into a dispute over what to do next. And some of Dan's best-laid plans begin to go awry. Take the vacation Bible school. The original plan for the week was that, every morning for a few hours, the kids would split into two different groups. Half would work on painting the Cunninghams' house, half would work on what they call a VBS, which is church-speak for Vacation Bible School, which itself is church-speak for trying to bring the word of God to local kids with games, skits, candy, a brief lesson.
<v Ira Glass>丹患上了某种食物中毒。当他住进医院时，这导致旅途中的四名成年领袖对下一步该怎么做产生了分歧。丹的一些精心策划开始偏离轨道。比如度假圣经学校。本周最初的计划是，每天早上几个小时，孩子们分成两组。一半人在坎宁安家粉刷房子，另一半人进行所谓的“VBS”（度假圣经学校的缩写，在教会语言中指通过游戏、短剧、糖果和简短的课程向当地孩子传播上帝的话语）。

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:32.220
<v Anna>What do we say?
<v Anna>我们怎么说？

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:35.550
<v Lauren>We say, we're doing a club. We're a youth group from Chicago.
<v Lauren>我们说，我们在办一个俱乐部。我们是来自芝加哥的青年团契。

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:39.880
<v Ira Glass>Anna and Lauren practice what they'll say to round up kids.
<v Ira Glass>安娜和劳伦练习如何召集孩子。

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:44.720
<v Lauren>If you have any kids-- should we ask if they have any kids? Or just say, if you have any kids-- if you're interested--
<v Lauren>如果你有孩子——我们该问他们是否有孩子吗？还是只说，如果你有任何孩子——如果你感兴趣——

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:46.070
<v Anna>Feel free to have them come.
<v Anna>请随时让他们过来。

00:18:46.070 --> 00:19:07.400
<v Ira Glass>But the group got bad advice about where to hold the vacation Bible school. We set up about a 10-minute drive from the Cunningham house in a big, empty public park. And as we went knocking from door to door to advertise the event to kids, it was like a fairy tale, where at each house, the person at the door was slightly more infirm, slightly more elderly, than the person at the house before.
<v Ira Glass>但小组关于在哪里举办度假圣经学校得到了糟糕的建议。我们在距离坎宁安家开车约10分钟路程的一个巨大的空旷公园里设立了场地。当我们挨家挨户敲门宣传这个活动时，就像童话故事一样，每一户人家门口的人都比上一户更虚弱、更年长。

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:11.620
<v Old Woman>I don't have any children. I don't have any grandchildren here and I don't know anybody that has kids.
<v Old Woman>我没有孩子。我这里没有孙子孙女，也不认识有孩子的人。

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:14.340
<v Girl 1>OK. Thank you.
<v Girl 1>好的。谢谢。

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:18.700
<v Girl 2>Hi, we were wondering if you had any kids that wanted to come to the backyard Bible club at Worthington Park.
<v Girl 2>嗨，我们想知道你是否有孩子想参加沃辛顿公园的后院圣经俱乐部。

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.650
<v Old Woman>I don't have any.
<v Old Woman>我没有。

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:26.510
<v Girl 2>Do you have any kids? Do you have-- do you have any children?
<v Girl 2>你有孩子吗？你有——你有任何孩子吗？

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:48.460
<v Ira Glass>And the next day, when we went out to hold the vacation Bible school, with props and costumes and games, with a pre-rehearsed skit and more candy than you might imagine, Karen, Marie, Jessica, Nora, Laura, Kelly, and Arden sat on the edge of an outdoor stage in the park, waiting. Nobody showed up. The closest they came was when a white pickup turned into the parking area.
<v Ira Glass>第二天，当我们带着道具、服装和游戏，准备好短剧和超乎想象的糖果去举办度假圣经学校时，凯伦、玛丽、杰西卡、诺拉、劳拉、凯莉和阿登坐在公园室外舞台的边缘等待。没有人来。最接近的一次是一辆白色皮卡开进了停车场。

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:51.470
<v Girl 1>Look it! Child! Child?
<v Girl 1>看！小孩！小孩？

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:55.600
<v Girl 2>Looks like it could be a family vehicle. [INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Girl 2>看起来像是家庭用车。[干扰的声音]

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:56.410
<v Girl 3>Oh, please!
<v Girl 3>哦，拜托！

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:57.910
<v Girl 4>Maybe they have seven kids.
<v Girl 4>也许他们有七个孩子。

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:00.560
<v Ira Glass>They didn't have seven. Or even one.
<v Ira Glass>他们没有七个。甚至一个都没有。

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:02.580
<v Girl 1>Oh well.
<v Girl 1>好吧。

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:08.760
<v Girl 2>So are we going to come back every day if nobody comes? Is this going to be like three hours of wasted time every day?
<v Girl 2>所以如果没人来，我们还要每天回来吗？这每天会不会是三个小时的浪费时间？

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:11.180
<v Girl 3>Two. Two hours.
<v Girl 3>两个小时。

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:15.730
<v Ira Glass>One of the group leaders, Nora, gathers them around.
<v Ira Glass>小组领袖之一诺拉把大家聚集在一起。

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:24.900
<v Nora>You know, I think that anyone who is led to pray should pray. Chris, can you start us off? And then Laura, can you just finish? Thanks.
<v Nora>你知道，我认为任何有感动祷告的人都应该祷告。克里斯，你能带个头吗？然后劳拉，你能结束吗？谢谢。

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:43.280
<v Ira Glass>They bow their heads. Here's one of the advantages of Christian life over secular life-- that at any moment of trouble, when somebody might get frustrated or mad, or for that matter, at any turning point in the day, when one thing ends and another's about to begin, our group takes a quiet moment and reflects.
<v Ira Glass>他们低下头。这是基督徒生活优于世俗生活的一个优势——在任何麻烦时刻，当有人可能感到沮丧或愤怒时，或者在那一天的任何转折点，当一件事结束、另一件事即将开始时，我们的小组会进行片刻安静的反思。

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:59.380
<v Chris>Dear Lord, we came here today, and we were really hoping that we'd be able to teach some kids about God, but it didn't work out that way. And maybe you're just showing us that it's better to go back to the house and work with the family there. But please show us some more signs.
<v Chris>亲爱的主，我们今天来到这里，真的很希望能教一些孩子认识你，但事情并没有如愿。也许你只是在向我们展示，回到房子里和那里的家人一起工作会更好。但请给我们展示更多的迹象。

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:30.210
<v Laura>Dear Lord, I want to thank you so much for this day, and that it was nice. And I just pray that we won't be discouraged that no kids will come. And that maybe-- well, you said that everything is worked for the good. And maybe we needed more time on the house, Lord. And I just pray that whatever happens, that good things will come out of this and we'll all do it for your glory.
<v Laura>亲爱的主，我非常感谢你赐予这一天，它很美好。我祈祷我们不会因为没人来而灰心。也许——嗯，你说过万事互相效力。也许我们在房子上需要更多的时间，主啊。我祈祷无论发生什么，好的结果都会从中产生，我们都会为了你的荣耀而做。

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:45.180
<v Ira Glass>Talking with the kids afterwards, I found that the lesson that they took from this experience was not that they could have chosen a better location or done a better job finding kids. It was not that they could have tried harder. What they all told me simply was, was that this was God's will.
<v Ira Glass>事后与孩子们交谈，我发现他们从这次经历中吸取的教训并不是他们本可以选择更好的地点或更努力地寻找孩子。也不是他们本可以更努力。他们简单地告诉我的，是这就是上帝的旨意。

00:21:45.180 --> 00:22:18.880
<v Ira Glass>It's a lesson that seems, to a secular person, strangely at odds with the job of raising children. It seems, in fact, to be exactly the opposite of what you would want to teach a child. Raising kids is, after all, a process of convincing them that they have to be responsible. That if something goes wrong, they have to figure out if there was anything they could have done to prevent it. I talked to Dan about this. I talked to the other adult leaders about it. And finally, the only person on the trip who says anything convincing about why this notion of God's will might be a good thing to teach teenagers is Ruth, the oldest girl in the youth group.
<v Ira Glass>对于一个世俗的人来说，这个教训似乎与抚养孩子的工作奇怪地不一致。事实上，这似乎恰恰与你想教给孩子的东西相反。毕竟，抚养孩子是一个让他们相信自己必须负责的过程。如果事情出了差错，他们必须找出是否有任何预防措施可以采取。我和丹谈过这个，和其他成年领袖也谈过。最后，这次旅行中唯一一个对为什么要教青少年上帝旨意这个概念给出令人信服解释的人，是青年团契中年龄最大的女孩露丝。

00:22:18.880 --> 00:23:27.090
<v Ruth>That helps me through the day. Because you see kids who don't care at all, you know, about Jesus and all of that, and they seem to be doing just fine. They seem to be doing a lot better. Because I'm in this really competitive program. And a lot of the kids are not Christians. And a lot of kids do really well. One of my classmates, I think, scored a perfect on the ACT. And I didn't. I mean, I wasn't very close. And I may take it over, I may not. The point is that, I don't know, they seem to be doing better. And sometimes I think, boy, I wish that I could do that well. And I guess it wasn't God's plan for me to do that well. It just seems like just knowing that, it's not all under our control, and that maybe that God does have a plan, so whatever He chooses-- though I may not like it, and I certainly don't like not coming out on top or not being the best.
<v Ruth>这能帮我度过每一天。因为你会看到那些根本不在乎耶稣和这一切的孩子，他们似乎过得很好。他们似乎做得更好。因为我参加的是一个竞争非常激烈的项目。很多孩子不是基督徒。而且很多孩子表现得非常好。我认为我的一位同学ACT得了满分。但我没有。我的意思是我差得远。我可能会重考，也可能不会。重点是，我不知道，他们似乎做得更好。有时我想，天哪，我希望我也能做得那么好。我想上帝对我做得那么好并不是他的计划。我猜，知道这并不是一切都在我们的控制之中，而且也许上帝确实有一个计划，无论他选择什么——尽管我可能不喜欢它，而且我当然不喜欢没能名列前茅或不是最棒的。

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:53.940
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Your Name Is Ointment Poured Forth. One great thing about staying in a camp with 130 other Christians, if you're a young Christian girl who has never had a boyfriend, is the much-better-than-in-school chance of meeting a nice, cute, Christian boy. Though, of course, camp rules prohibit anything more than having a crush from afar. This turns out not to cramp the style of the Chicago girls, however. A crush from afar is exactly as far as they could handle.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，你的膏油芬芳。如果你是一个从未交过男朋友的年轻基督徒女孩，住在有130名其他基督徒的营地里，有一个远比在学校里见到可爱、帅气的基督徒男孩的机会，这是一件伟大的事情。当然，营地规则禁止除了远距离倾慕以外的任何行为。然而，事实证明这并没有限制住芝加哥女孩们的风格。远距离倾慕正是她们所能处理的极限。

00:23:53.940 --> 00:24:08.550
<v Ira Glass>Monday night, after services and after devotions, everybody is milling around the volleyball court in the center of camp. There are boys and girls there. And one of the adult leaders, Nora, comes down carrying a word game for everybody to play. A word game. What could be more embarrassing?
<v Ira Glass>周一晚上，在礼拜和灵修之后，每个人都在营地中心的排球场附近徘徊。那里有男孩也有女孩。其中一位成年领袖诺拉拿来了一个供大家玩的文字游戏。一个文字游戏。还有比这更尴尬的吗？

