<v Ira Glass>他的口音在解释时流露出梅森-迪克森线以下的味道。

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:36.470
<v Dan Adamson>We actually have a little present for you. Because we knew it was going to be your birthday, so one of the girls went out and got a little present for you. So. Well, if you guys ever get bored and want to come over there, there's a bunch of people. We're going to be here all week. We'd love to talk to you. We got some candy and stuff. Introduce yourself to them. And if you want to come by, we're going to be eating lunch. What time do we eat lunch?
<v Dan Adamson>我们其实给你准备了一个小礼物。因为我们知道那是你的生日，所以其中一个女孩出去给你买了个小礼物。所以。好吧，如果你们感到无聊想过来看看，那里有一群人。我们要在这里待一整周。我们很乐意和你们交谈。我们有一些糖果之类的。向他们介绍一下自己。如果你们想过来，我们午饭时间要在那儿吃。我们什么时候吃午饭？

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:37.320
<v Marisa>Like 12:00.
<v Marisa>大概12点吧。

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:40.970
<v Dan Adamson>12:00. We got some-- you can eat with us. We've got some candy and food and drinks.
<v Dan Adamson>12点。我们有——你们可以和我们一起吃。我们有一些糖果、食物和饮料。

00:32:40.970 --> 00:33:01.800
<v Ira Glass>Back next door, everybody works on the Cunninghams' house. Scraping off the old paint, rolling on the new, trimming back bushes, climbing up ladders. Everybody dressed in surgical scrubs, those blue-green cotton pajamas that you see the doctors wear on ER. That is, if your faith allows you to watch ER. They're light, they're disposable, and they let you call each other doctor all day long.
<v Ira Glass>在隔壁，大家都在坎宁安家工作。刮掉旧油漆，刷上新的，修剪灌木，爬上梯子。每个人都穿着手术洗手服，就是你在《急诊室的故事》里看到医生穿的那种蓝绿色棉质睡衣。前提是你的信仰允许你看《急诊室的故事》。它们很轻、是一次性的，还能让你整天互相称呼“医生”。

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:13.780
<v Ira Glass>The work goes quickly. At some point, to dislodge some ancient paint off aluminum siding, the adults drag in an industrial power washer that they warn the kids about so frighteningly, some get sort of spooked.
<v Ira Glass>工作进行得很快。在某个时候，为了从铝壁板上清除古老的油漆，大人们搬来了一个工业动力清洗机，他们警告孩子们要小心，甚至吓到了某些人。

00:33:13.780 --> 00:33:20.650
<v Girl>I don't know. I'm afraid to use the power hose thing. Because I feel like I'm going to kill somebody. And I mean seriously kill somebody.
<v Girl>我不知道。我害怕使用那个高压软管的东西。因为我觉得我会杀了某人。我是说真的杀了某人。

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:25.560
<v Ira Glass>Lindsay stands in line, deciding if she'll take a turn.
<v Ira Glass>琳赛排着队，决定是否要轮到她。

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:37.050
<v Lindsay>I don't want to see it.
<v Lindsay>我不想看。

00:33:37.050 --> 00:33:42.140
<v Ira Glass>Once the power washer gets going, Ruth is the first one to use it to actually remove paint.
<v Ira Glass>一旦动力清洗机开始工作，露丝是第一个真正用它清除油漆的人。

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:48.430
<v Ruth>It's fun, yeah. I'm the oldest, so I like the power.
<v Ruth>这很有趣，是的。我是最大的，所以我喜欢这种力量。

00:33:48.430 --> 00:34:14.510
<v Ira Glass>Among an earlier generation of Christian women, or among more fundamentalist Christian women, there might be some question about whether it is a woman's place to run the power tools in the family. This question is so irrelevant to these Presbyterian teenagers that when I bring it up they literally do not know what I'm talking about.
<v Ira Glass>在老一代基督徒女性中，或者在更原教旨主义的基督徒女性中，对于女性在家庭中操作电动工具是否合宜可能存在疑问。这些长老会的青少年对这个问题完全不了解，当我提起时，她们简直不知道我在说什么。

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:39.410
<v Ira Glass>While they all work on the house, every now and then somebody from the Cunningham family is spotted. And every single time, it is like the experience that you have when you spot a minor celebrity. Your heart leaps. It's all you can think about. And then, if you're brave enough to stroll over and chat, you try to play it casual. Like, oh, we're just talking here. I'm relaxed. It's just like talking to anybody. After all, they are here to minister to this family.
<v Ira Glass>当她们都在房子上工作时，偶尔会看到坎宁安家的人。每次看到时，那种感觉就像你偶然发现一个小名人一样。你的心跳加速。你满脑子想的都是这个。然后，如果你勇敢地走过去闲聊，你会尽量表现得随和。就像，哦，我们只是在这聊天。我很放松。这就像和任何人交谈一样。毕竟，她们是来服务这个家庭的。

00:34:39.410 --> 00:35:01.990
<v Ira Glass>When the Cunninghams' daughter, Angie, comes out on the porch one afternoon, Kelly and Jessica do their best to be friendly. And for once, Kelly's mother might feel like she did the right thing buying her daughter all those Backstreet Boy CDs. Because now they get the chance to talk Backstreet Boys on the Lord's behalf. Paula and Crystal, the girls from next door, bring over a Backstreet Boys poster. Angie owns a t-shirt from the '98 tour.
<v Ira Glass>一天下午，当坎宁安家的女儿安吉走上门廊时，凯莉和杰西卡尽力表现得友好。而这一次，凯莉的母亲可能会觉得给女儿买那些后街男孩的CD是正确的做法。因为现在她们有机会代表主来谈论后街男孩。邻居家的女孩宝拉和克里斯托拿来了一张后街男孩的海报。安吉拥有一件98年巡演的T恤。

00:35:01.990 --> 00:35:28.980
<v Ira Glass>Angie is actually in her late 20s. She has a small disability in one of her arms. It's shorter than the other and her hand on that arm isn't fully functional. Most days she stays at home, watching TV. It is inherently difficult for the Chicago teenagers to figure out how to interact with, even how to react to, somebody from such a different background. And there's a certain amount of raw confusion as they feel their way forward. Every night back at camp, after prayers and devotionals, they all debrief about the day.
<v Ira Glass>安吉其实已经快30岁了。她的一只手臂有轻微的残疾。它比另一只短，而且那只手的功能也不完全。大部分时间她都待在家里看电视。对于芝加哥青少年来说，弄清楚如何与背景如此不同的人互动，甚至如何反应，是极其困难的。在她们摸索前进时，有一定程度的原始困惑。每晚回到营地，在祷告和灵修之后，她们都会汇报当天的进展。

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:33.300
<v Dan Adamson>So anyone have any thoughts about today? Positive, negative feedback? Anything?
<v Dan Adamson>那么有人对今天有什么想法吗？正面、负面的反馈？有什么吗？

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:36.210
<v Ira Glass>Kelly uses the chance to speak up.
<v Ira Glass>凯莉趁机发言。

00:35:36.210 --> 00:36:23.010
<v Kelly Hoover>I thought, at first, it was kind of hard to talk to Angie, just because, I mean, I didn't like staring. I mean, I didn't want to. And it's not just because of her arm. It's because it is hard to understand her, and she didn't really get what you said sometimes. But at first I was kind of doing it out of pity for her, and then that didn't feel right, you know? I didn't want to do it out of pity for her. And I don't know if it's wrong or right to do it out of pity for her. But that's how I'm doing it. And I don't know if that's right. I just-- and it makes me so happy when I see her smile. And it's just-- I guess I don't know if it's right or wrong to do it out of pity. I know I keep asking that. So someone answer.
<v Kelly Hoover>起初，我觉得和安吉说话有点困难，因为——我的意思是，我不想盯着看。我的意思是不只是因为她的手臂。是因为很难理解她，有时她没听懂你在说什么。但起初我有点出于同情她才那么做，然后这感觉不太对，你知道吗？我不想出于同情才做。我不知道出于同情这么做是错还是对。但我就是这么做的。而且我不知道这是否正确。我只是——看到她微笑时我感到很高兴。而且这只是——我猜我不知道出于同情做这件事是对还是错。我知道我一直在问这个问题。所以有人回答一下。

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:36.370
<v Ira Glass>Nobody does for a while, until later, when Dan says the perfect thing, that when Jesus walked the earth, he didn't feel pity for the sick and handicapped. He felt compassion, empathy. Lauren offers this optimistic thought.
<v Ira Glass>没人回答，直到后来，丹说出了那句完美的话：当耶稣走在地上时，他并没有对病残者感到“同情”（怜悯），而是感到了同情（共情）。劳伦提出了这个乐观的想法。

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:46.220
<v Lauren>She obviously has a lot of problems with her deformity and whatever. And I was just thinking, in heaven, she's not going to have any deformity. And if we can reach her that way--
<v Lauren>她显然因为残疾或其他原因有很多问题。我刚才在想，在天堂里，她不会有任何残疾。如果我们能以那种方式接触到她——

00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:55.070
<v Ira Glass>Over and over, Dan tells the group that their main purpose in coming to West Virginia is to minister to the family whose house they're working on. Getting to know Angie is important, he says.
<v Ira Glass>丹反复告诉小组成员，他们来西弗吉尼亚州的主要目的是服务他们正在修缮房子的那个家庭。他说，了解安吉很重要。

00:36:55.070 --> 00:37:35.840
<v Dan Adamson>Remember the names. I know I need it. I kept telling Carol, Carol, what were their names again? Really, they love that they're going to know their names. I thought it was great you guys were showing your boy bands. I thought that was great. I thought that was great. And they say they go to that church. I asked them if they have youth group events and they said they've done a couple youth group events. So I think, you know, if the timing is right, I think you have a good opportunity to just ask them, well, what do you think about God? I mean, I don't think it would be awkward at all. I just think I would encourage you, and be praying about it, that if you feel like the motivation is there, man, just plant a seed. Just see what happens, and maybe you're going to go with it.
<v Dan Adamson>记住名字。我知道我需要。我一直告诉卡罗尔，卡罗尔，她们的名字叫什么来着？真的，她们喜欢别人知道她们的名字。我觉得你们展示男孩乐队很棒。我觉得这很好。我觉得这很好。她们说她们去那个教堂。我问她们是否参加过青年团契活动，她们说参加过几个。所以我认为，你知道，如果时机成熟，我认为你们有很好的机会问她们，嗯，你们怎么看上帝？我的意思是，我觉得这根本不尴尬。我只是想鼓励你们，并为此祷告，如果你们觉得动机存在，伙计，种下一颗种子。看看会发生什么，也许你会顺其自然。

00:37:35.840 --> 00:38:10.650
<v Ira Glass>Listening to all this, I imagine how disturbed Angie and Crystal and Paula might be if they overheard all the scheming of how they'll be befriended. From their point of view, after all, their interaction with the Chicago girls is as simple as, they seem sort of cool. They like them. But missionary work is, at some level, like any sales work. There's no way to do it without discussing some sort of sales strategy. All of these evening sessions end with prayer, in which anybody who wants to say something simply speaks up. And a lot of the prayers are about Angie, and the two neighbors, Crystal and Paula. Kelly was among those offering a prayer.
<v Ira Glass>听着这一切，我想象着如果安吉、克里斯托和宝拉听到所有关于如何与她们建立友谊的密谋，会感到多么不安。毕竟在她们看来，她们与芝加哥女孩们的互动很简单：她们看起来挺酷的。她们喜欢她们。但宣教工作在某种程度上像任何销售工作。不讨论某种销售策略是不可能的。所有这些晚间会议都以祷告结束，任何想说点什么的人都会发言。很多祷告都是关于安吉以及那两个邻居克里斯托和宝拉的。凯莉也在祈祷者之中。

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:20.960
<v Kelly Hoover>I just pray that tomorrow we'd really get to talk to Mrs. Cunningham and Angela and just really be a witness for you, Lord.
<v Kelly Hoover>我祈祷明天我们真的能和坎宁安夫人和安吉谈谈，真正为主做一个见证，主啊。

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:34.370
<v Woman>I especially pray for Angela, and just help us to ask the right questions, and to think of things to say and talk about with her. And to find out exactly what makes her tick inside and what makes her special.
<v Woman>我特别为安吉祈祷，帮助我们问出正确的问题，想到可以说和谈论的事情。找出她内心深处到底是什么让她动心，什么让她变得特别。

00:38:34.370 --> 00:38:47.780
<v Girl>Pray for Angela, that she'll just really get to know us. That we won't be like every other group and that we'll just really, really get to know her as a really good friend.
<v Girl>为安吉祈祷，她真的能了解我们。我们不会像其他小组一样，我们真的，真的能和她成为很好的朋友。

00:38:47.780 --> 00:39:00.990
<v Ira Glass>And from there, everybody headed to the volleyball court, where-- I am sorry to report-- the Chicagoans lost. Lauren's second serve went straight to the guy she sort of had a crush on, and he spiked it like he didn't even know she existed.
<v Ira Glass>从那里，每个人都前往排球场，在那里——我遗憾地报告——芝加哥队输了。劳伦的第二个发球直奔她暗恋的那个男孩，他像根本不知道她存在一样扣杀了它。

00:39:00.990 --> 00:39:05.480
<v Girl>Come on, Lindsay!
<v Girl>得了吧，琳赛！

00:39:05.480 --> 00:39:12.270
<v Ira Glass>Susan, meanwhile, is watching the volleyball game from the sidelines when those two guys, Matt and Eric, who want to be on the radio, show right up.
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，苏珊在场边观看排球比赛，那两个想上电台的家伙，马特和埃里克出现了。

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:15.390
<v Matt>All right, the game is getting very exciting at this point.
<v Matt>好吧，比赛现在变得非常激动人心。

00:39:15.390 --> 00:39:17.130
<v Susan Burton>Yeah, why don't you guys narrate the rest of the game for me, OK?
<v Susan Burton>是的，你们为什么不帮我解说剩下的比赛呢，好吗？

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:35.790
<v Eric>All right. It has been-- [INTERPOSING VOICES]. Ball returned to the Chicago team and Chicago returns with a bunt over the net! It is returned! And it is on! Chicago has done it! Oh! [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Eric>好的。这已经——[干扰的声音]。球传回给芝加哥队，芝加哥队以一个过网的轻吊回击！球被接回了！比赛继续！芝加哥队做到了！哦！[无法听清]

00:39:35.790 --> 00:39:40.440
<v Ira Glass>Afterwards, naturally, they insist that-- guess what?-- this has changed them.
<v Ira Glass>事后，自然地，他们坚持认为——猜猜怎么着？——这改变了他们。

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:45.720
<v Eric>This game just now has made us appreciate volleyball even less than we ever did before.
<v Eric>刚才这场比赛让我们对排球的欣赏比以往任何时候都少。

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:53.360
<v Matt>--on the American volleyball team. I'm going to be the official spiker.
<v Matt>——在美式排球队。我要成为官方扣球手。

00:39:53.360 --> 00:40:00.800
<v Ira Glass>The evening ended the way every other evening did on the trip. In the cabin, the Chicago crew sang a doxology and then one other song.
<v Ira Glass>夜晚像旅途中的每一个夜晚一样结束。在小屋里，芝加哥队唱了一首荣耀颂，然后唱了另一首歌。

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:06.250
<v Multiple Girls>[SINGING] When I say that I want it that way.
<v Multiple Girls>[演唱] 当我说我想要那样时。

00:40:06.250 --> 00:40:07.750
<v Ira Glass>The Backstreet Boys, "Tell Me Why."
<v Ira Glass>后街男孩的《Tell Me Why》。

00:40:07.750 --> 00:40:19.980
<v Multiple Girls>[SINGING] Tell me why, ain't nothin' but a heartache. Tell me why. Ain't nothin' but a mistake--
<v Multiple Girls>[演唱] 告诉我为什么，这只是心痛。告诉我为什么。这只是个错误——

00:40:19.980 --> 00:40:23.730
<v Ira Glass>Act Six, The Evidence of Things Not Seen.
<v Ira Glass>第六幕，所不见之事的证据。

00:40:23.730 --> 00:40:29.070
<v Dan Adamson>Well, if any of your buddies live around here that didn't get candy, you let us know and we'll get them some.
<v Dan Adamson>好吧，如果你们住在这里的朋友没拿到糖果，让我们知道，我们会给他们一些。

00:40:29.070 --> 00:40:35.960
<v Ira Glass>This is Dan, trying to attract kids to vacation Bible school, literally by walking down the street and passing out handfuls of candy.
<v Ira Glass>这是丹，试图通过走在大街上分发糖果来吸引孩子们参加度假圣经学校。

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:36.390
<v Dan Adamson>You guys just been hanging out?
<v Dan Adamson>你们刚才一直在闲逛？

00:40:36.390 --> 00:40:38.290
<v Ira Glass>Which turns out to be surprisingly effective.
<v Ira Glass>这结果证明出人意料地有效。

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:43.570
<v Dan Adamson>Is this your brother? You got some candy, too, Christopher? Excellent.
<v Dan Adamson>这是你弟弟吗？你也拿了一些糖果吗，克里斯托弗？太棒了。

00:40:43.570 --> 00:41:06.170
<v Ira Glass>The idea is give up on their first location where nobody showed up for Bible school. Just do it around the corner from the house they're painting. And what clinched the whole deal, the two teenage girls from next door, Crystal and Paula, took Dan and the Chicago kids from door to door, rounding up prospects. In all, about a dozen kids showed up Thursday morning for games, t-shirt painting, and a lesson from the Bible presented in the form of a skit.
<v Ira Glass>主意是放弃第一个没人参加圣经学校的地点。就在我们要粉刷的房子的拐角处进行。最终促成这一切的是隔壁的两个少女，克里斯托和宝拉，她们带着丹和芝加哥的孩子们挨家挨户敲门，寻找潜在参与者。总共大约有十几名孩子周四早上来参加游戏、T恤绘画，并听了一个以短剧形式呈现的圣经课。

00:41:06.170 --> 00:41:23.170
<v Dan Adamson>So let's everyone close their eyes and we're going to pray. God, we thank you for this day. We thank you for everyone that made it here today. And I pray that we can have a fun time now, we can learn through your skit, and then we could also enjoy doing our craft and playing some more games, and having a snack. In your precious name, amen. Now, with no further ado--
<v Dan Adamson>那么让我们每个人闭上眼睛开始祷告。神啊，我们感谢你这一天。我们感谢今天所有来到这里的人。我祈祷我们现在能玩得开心，我们可以通过短剧学习，然后我们也可以享受做手工、多做游戏和吃零食的快乐。奉你宝贵的名，阿们。现在，不用多说——

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:47.430
<v Ira Glass>The whole Bible school turns out to be really fun, for the West Virginia kids and the Chicago ones, too. The games go over great. The crafts absorb everybody. Some of the kids clearly have hard lives and are excited at all the attention they're getting. The only thing lacking is nonbelievers. All the kids say they go to church. When it comes time to make beaded faith bracelets, they already know what all the beads stand for. During Dan's homily, most of the kids know the Bible stories he tells.
<v Ira Glass>整个圣经学校证明真的很有趣，对于西弗吉尼亚州的孩子和芝加哥的孩子来说都是如此。游戏效果很好。手工吸引了每个人。有些孩子显然生活艰辛，对他们得到的关注感到兴奋。唯一缺少的是非信徒。所有的孩子都说他们去教堂。当制作珠子信仰手链时，他们已经知道所有珠子代表什么。在丹的布道期间，大多数孩子都知道他讲的圣经故事。

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:52.540
<v Dan Adamson>You guys know the story about the Red Sea, though, right? Well, here's a good story about the Red Sea.
<v Dan Adamson>不过你们都知道关于红海的故事，对吧？好吧，这是一个关于红海的好故事。

00:41:52.540 --> 00:41:54.050
<v Boy>He just tapped the water and--
<v Boy>他只是拍了拍水，然后——

00:41:54.050 --> 00:41:55.030
<v Dan Adamson>Right, tapped the water and what happened?
<v Dan Adamson>对了，拍了拍水，发生了什么？

00:41:55.030 --> 00:41:56.130
<v Boy>It split.
<v Boy>它分开了。

00:41:56.130 --> 00:42:22.370
<v Ira Glass>Later, Carol and Marisa are kneeling in the grass doing Bible study when Angie and Crystal just walk right up to them. This is pretty much a situation that any missionary would regard as ideal. And, having no choice, as if a greater plan is, in fact, at work, the Chicagoans get down to the hardcore work of talking about religion. Crystal goes home to get her Bible. One of the adult leaders, Carol, does most of the talking, with Jessica and Kelly and Marisa piping in from time to time.
<v Ira Glass>后来，卡罗尔和玛丽莎跪在草地上查经时，安吉和克里斯托径直走了过来。这几乎是任何宣教士都会认为理想的情况。于是，别无选择，就好像有一个更大的计划在运作，芝加哥人开始进行谈论宗教的艰苦工作。克里斯托回家拿她的圣经。成年领袖之一卡罗尔说得最多，杰西卡、凯莉和玛丽莎不时插嘴。

00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:36.980
<v Carol>Does Paula have a Bible, too, that she reads? Yeah? So that's interesting that your school gave you a Bible. Is it like-- [INTERPOSING VOICES] a religious school? Your school did it, too. Is it a religious school? Do they talk about God at school?
<v Carol>宝拉也有她读的圣经吗？是吗？所以很有趣，你们学校给了你一本圣经。是——[干扰的声音]一所宗教学校吗？你们学校也做了。是一所宗教学校吗？他们在学校谈论上帝吗？

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:37.550
<v Crystal>No.
<v Crystal>没有。

00:42:37.550 --> 00:42:41.810
<v Carol>No? They just gave you a Bible? That's kind of cool.
<v Carol>没有？他们只是给了你一本圣经？那挺酷的。

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:49.550
<v Girl>Yeah, if any of the public schools gave us a Bible in Chicago, they'd be in trouble. They're not allowed to do that.
<v Girl>是的，如果芝加哥的公立学校给我们圣经，他们会有麻烦的。他们不允许那样做。

00:42:49.550 --> 00:42:55.960
<v Ira Glass>During breaks, all the girls chat happily with the neighborhood kids. And later, back at camp, Dan congratulated them all.
<v Ira Glass>休息期间，女孩们和社区的孩子们聊得很开心。后来，回到营地，丹祝贺了她们所有人。

00:42:55.960 --> 00:43:25.580
<v Dan Adamson>You guys did so well today. I'm so proud of you, of how you guys were. All of you just seemed to-- really, when you saw them, you were very friendly. With Paula, Crystal, and with Angie. Thank you so much. And let's keep that up tomorrow. This is a great-- I mean, I would say that, of all the people, including all the VBS kids that we've had, the most seeds that we've been planting have been with those three girls.
<v Dan Adamson>你们今天做得太好了。我为你们感到骄傲，关于你们的表现。你们所有人都似乎——真的，当你看到她们时，你们非常友好。对待宝拉、克里斯托和安吉。非常感谢。明天让我们继续保持。这是一个伟大的——我的意思是，我会说，在所有人中，包括我们所有的VBS孩子，我们一直在播下的种子最多的是那三个女孩。

00:43:25.580 --> 00:43:58.610
<v Ira Glass>It had been Dan's hope that on this trip, the group's faith would deepen. That they would make a step toward having a faith that was independent of what their parents taught them. This would partly happen through service, by helping others, and partly by being forced to articulate their faith to others. And it would happen by facing hardship on the trip and turning to God to help with the difficulties. But the fact is the hardships on the trip turned out to be not very hard at all, and most of them only had to articulate their faith to six-year-olds, who had mostly already accepted Jesus into their hearts. This was not enough to launch a round of soul searching and prayer.
<v Ira Glass>丹曾希望这次旅行能深化小组的信仰。他们能迈出走向拥有独立于父母所教导的信仰的一步。这部分通过服务、通过帮助他人，部分通过被迫向他人表达信仰来实现。而且这将通过在旅途中面对苦难并转向神寻求帮助来完成。但事实是，旅途中的艰辛并没有那么困难，她们中的大多数人只需要向六岁的孩子表达信仰，而这些孩子大多已经接受耶稣进入了他们的心里。这不足以引发一轮自我审视和祷告。

00:43:58.610 --> 00:44:12.350
<v Ira Glass>Instead, what happened was that the kids who didn't doubt their faith at home didn't doubt it here either. And the kids who had all sorts of questions at home continued to gnaw at those questions on the mission trip. The teenager with the most doubts was Arden, who mostly kept her doubts to herself.
<v Ira Glass>相反，发生的事情是，那些在家不怀疑自己信仰的孩子在这里也没有怀疑。而那些在家有各种问题的孩子在宣教之旅中继续咀嚼这些问题。怀疑最多的是阿登，她大多把疑虑藏在心里。

00:44:12.350 --> 00:44:27.350
<v Arden>Which is really, it's frustrating. It makes it hard. To be-- wanting to be part of the group, and being part of the group, and yet knowing that in some way you're not part of the group because you question what the group is about.
<v Arden>这真的很令人沮丧。这让一切变得艰难。想要成为小组的一员，并且身在其中，但又知道自己在某种程度上不是小组的一员，因为你质疑这个小组的意义。

00:44:27.350 --> 00:45:07.380
<v Ira Glass>Arden holds a special symbolic place in her church. She was the first baby born to the young congregation, 15 years ago. Whenever the kids in church formed their own ad hoc church youth groups, Arden was always president. But about a year ago, even this carefully raised girl started to grapple with doubt. And her doubt began, not because of something she saw on television, not because of peer pressure from attractive, cigarette-smoking, alcohol-drinking, pornographic-website-using fans of the South Park movie. No. Her doubts began at church camp. She was there for six weeks, at a program called Discipleship Training, which sent her and five girls that she'd never met before into the woods for two weeks of camping and hiking and canoeing together.
<v Ira Glass>阿登在她的教会中拥有一个特殊的象征性地位。她是15年前这个年轻会众出生的第一个婴儿。每当教会里的孩子组成自己的临时教会青年团契时，阿登总是主席。但大约一年前，即使是这个被精心抚养的女孩也开始挣扎于怀疑。她的怀疑并非源于她在电视上看到的东西，并非源于那些有吸引力、抽烟、酗酒、看色情网站的《南方公园》电影粉丝的同伴压力。不，她的怀疑始于教会营地。她在那里待了六周，参加一个名为“门徒训练”的项目，该项目把她和五个她以前从未见过的女孩送进树林，进行了两周的露营、徒步旅行和划独木舟。

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:25.770
<v Arden>And we were really tight. I mean, we just did everything together. We prayed and we would sing songs together at night. And when we'd get lost, I mean, the first thing we'd do would be like, it's time to pray. And when we got to Lake Superior at midnight, we were like, it's time to pray. We were just really-- I mean, it was a wonderful experience. I had a lot of fun.
<v Arden>我们真的很亲密。我的意思是，我们什么都一起做。我们祷告，晚上一起唱歌。当我们迷路时，我的意思是，我们做的第一件事就是，是时候祷告了。当我们午夜到达苏必利尔湖时，我们就像，是时候祷告了。我们真的很——我的意思是，那是一次美妙的经历。我玩得很开心。

00:45:25.770 --> 00:45:31.430
<v Ira Glass>And if you had to describe your feelings about God during those first two weeks, how would you describe Him?
<v Ira Glass>如果你必须描述你在头两周对上帝的感受，你会如何描述他？

00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:39.980
<v Arden>He was incredibly real. I felt like He was there. That was the only way I could describe it, is that He was really there for me.
<v Arden>他非常真实。我觉得他在那里。我唯一能描述的方式就是，他真的在那里为我。

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:45.200
<v Ira Glass>After two weeks, Arden and her five new friends came in from the woods to rejoin the rest of the camp.
<v Ira Glass>两周后，阿登和她的五个新朋友从树林里出来，重新加入营地的其他人。

00:45:45.200 --> 00:46:53.320
<v Arden>My friends suddenly changed, like who I'd thought they were-- they suddenly became different. And I don't think-- I think it was just that I hadn't seen them with other people besides our group. And they were-- they would-- it all became-- you know, there was the popular one, and then there was the really helpful one, and there was the boy chaser. I mean, it was just like, I didn't know how to handle it. I was still hoping to have that intimate friendship among the five of us. And I thought I was going to die. I just couldn't take it. And then I sort of dropped into my spiritual low. And so I started having trouble praying. I didn't want to pray. And I was starting go, well, you know, what's up with this? What is real? If I can't even-- when I feel like God's there, and then I suddenly feel like He's not, where is He this whole time? You know? So that's when I really started to question. And I had all these doubts and I felt like God wasn't anywhere near me at all and He didn't care.
<v Arden>我的朋友们突然变了，就像我以为她们是谁——她们突然变得不一样了。我不认为——我认为只是因为我没有在我们的群体之外看到过她们与其他人在一起。她们是——她们会——这一切都变成了——你知道，有受欢迎的那一个，有非常乐于助人的那一个，还有追逐男孩的那一个。我的意思是，这就像，我不知道该怎么处理。我仍然希望我们五个之间能有那种亲密的友谊。我觉得我要死了。我简直受不了。然后我陷入了灵性的低谷。所以我开始在祷告上遇到麻烦。我不想祷告。我开始问，好吧，你知道，这是怎么回事？什么是真实的？如果我甚至不能——当我感觉到上帝在那里，然后我突然觉得他不在时，他一直都在哪？你知道吗？所以那是真正开始质疑的时候。我有所有的怀疑，我觉得上帝根本不在我身边，他也不关心。

00:46:53.320 --> 00:47:34.170
<v Ira Glass>One of the traditions in Christianity is that great faith often comes only after great doubt. The New Testament is filled with one story after another like this. Even Jesus grapples with his conscience in the desert before he begins his ministry. He prays again in Gethsemane. And listening to Arden, I begin to wonder if all new believers have to go through that trial in the desert themselves if they're going to arrive at a mature faith. This is what Dan and a number of the parents believe will keep Christianity as part of these teenagers' lives as they grow older. But it is enormously difficult to go through this kind of doubt. It makes Arden unhappy. She says she does not want another big spiritual moment, feeling close to God, like she had in the woods.
<v Ira Glass>基督教的传统之一是，伟大的信仰往往只在伟大的怀疑之后才会到来。新约充满了这样一个接一个的故事。即使是耶稣在开始他的侍奉之前，也在沙漠中与良心搏斗。他在客西马尼园再次祷告。听着阿登的话，我开始想，如果所有新信徒想要获得成熟的信仰，是否都必须亲自经历沙漠中的那种试炼。这就是丹和一些父母认为可以让基督教随着这些青少年成长而继续成为他们生活一部分的原因。但经历这种怀疑是非常困难的。这让阿登不开心。她说她不想要另一个重大的属灵时刻，感觉像她在树林里那样亲近上帝。

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:41.090
<v Arden>I don't trust that anymore. I don't trust, you know, just your emotions, and where they may take you.
<v Arden>我不再相信那个了。我不相信，你知道，仅仅你的情绪，以及它们可能带你走向哪里。

00:47:41.090 --> 00:47:47.000
<v Ira Glass>And at the same time, she knows that without a pure leap of faith, she's nowhere.
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，她知道没有纯粹的信心之跃，她就一无所有。

00:47:47.000 --> 00:48:10.440
<v Arden>That's the thing that worries me the most, is that I may never find what I'm looking for. I may never find out who God is. And that worries me. Like sometimes I think, oh my gosh, you know, because I don't know where I would be. I don't know. I'd have to start all over again.
<v Arden>这是最让我担心的事情，是我可能永远找不到我在寻找的东西。我可能永远找不到上帝是谁。这让我很担心。有时候我想，天哪，你知道，因为我不知道我会去哪里。我不知道。我得从头再来。

00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:26.550
<v Pastor Kenny>Abraham our father answered his Hineni when the Lord commanded him to offer up his son. He took the wood and fire and journeyed to Moriah, and there he built an altar to complete what he'd begun.
<v Pastor Kenny>我们的祖先亚伯拉罕在主命令他献出儿子时回答了“我在”。他带着木柴和火，前往摩利亚山，在那里他建造了一座祭坛以完成他开始的事业。

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:54.370
<v Ira Glass>Pastor Kenny actually broke into song during his sermon on Thursday. The subject of the sermon was how the Lord calls certain people to serve him, and how when he does, they usually do not want to go. We hear the story of Abraham being called to slay Isaac, of Moses being ordered to go to Pharaoh. Kenny talks from the heart about the moment he felt he was called to preach. And then he asks all of us to look into our own hearts. Has God been speaking to us during our week in West Virginia?
<v Ira Glass>肯尼牧师在周四的布道中竟然唱了起来。布道的主题是主如何呼召特定的人来侍奉他，以及当他这样做时，他们通常不想去。我们听到了亚伯拉罕被呼召去杀以撒，摩西被命令去见法老的故事。肯尼发自内心地谈到了他感到自己被呼召去布道的那一刻。然后他让我们所有人审视自己的内心。我们在西弗吉尼亚州的这一周，上帝有没有在对我们说话？

00:48:54.370 --> 00:49:34.360
<v Pastor Kenny>Maybe God is speaking to you. That you want to be a missionary. Now over here under my Bible, I've got a legal pad. And if you think that God is calling you to serve Him, maybe that's what God wants you to do. I'm just going to leave this pad out here and I want you to just come put your name on it. I'm not going to sell your name to any list. I'm not going to turn it in to Mission to the World. This is between-- I think you need to do that. You need to make that commitment, if God is calling you.
<v Pastor Kenny>也许上帝正在对你说话。你想成为一名宣教士。在我圣经下面这儿，我有一本便签纸。如果你认为上帝在呼召你侍奉他，也许这就是上帝希望你做的事。我只是把这张纸放在这儿，我希望你们能过来写上名字。我不会把你们的名字卖给任何名单。我也不会把它交给“使命世界”。这是你们和——我认为你们需要做那个。如果上帝在呼召你，你需要做出那个承诺。

00:49:34.360 --> 00:49:48.740
<v Ira Glass>After the service ends, a few people mill around. The Chicago teenagers head off to the cabins, except for Joel, who walks over to the legal pad, picks it up, and writes his name. I catch up with him a few minutes later.
<v Ira Glass>礼拜结束后，一些人在周围徘徊。芝加哥的青少年们前往小屋，除了乔尔，他走向便签纸，拿起笔，写下了他的名字。几分钟后我追上了他。

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:50.610
<v Ira Glass>So I saw what you just did.
<v Ira Glass>所以我看到了你刚才做的。

00:49:50.610 --> 00:49:58.760
<v Joel>Yeah, I signed the list. It was-- for me-- to me-- it was the most powerful sermon I've ever heard.
<v Joel>是的，我在名单上签名了。对于我来说——对我来说——这是我听过的最有力的布道。

00:49:58.760 --> 00:50:20.370
<v Ira Glass>Joel tells me he now knows what he wants to do with his life. Although parts of his faith clearly still chafe at him, although he's always saying how hard it is for him to talk to people, although he still has a long way to go before he's actually at ease in the bigger secular world, he's the one member of the youth group who decided that he wants to head out into that world, as a missionary teaching in another country.
<v Ira Glass>乔尔告诉我，他现在知道了他想用自己的余生做什么。尽管他信仰的某些部分显然仍然让他感到不舒服，尽管他总是说和人交谈有多难，尽管他在真正自在地融入更广阔的世俗世界之前还有很长的路要走，但他还是青年团契中唯一一个决定想进入那个世界，作为一个去另一个国家教书的宣教士的成员。

00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:32.930
<v Joel>When Kenny was praying, I was just like, man, what have you been thinking all these years? No, I'm not going to be a professional soccer player. That's totally out now.
<v Joel>当肯尼祷告时，我心里想，伙计，这些年来你都在想些什么？不，我不会成为职业足球运动员了。那现在完全不行了。

00:50:32.930 --> 00:50:34.920
<v Ira Glass>Is that what you thought up until that point?
<v Ira Glass>在那之前你是这么想的吗？

00:50:34.920 --> 00:50:37.890
<v Joel>Yeah, it is.
<v Joel>是的，确实。

00:50:37.890 --> 00:50:49.320
<v Ira Glass>Given how restrictive-- I hope you don't take this the wrong way-- but given how restrictive your family is, I can see why you might jump at a chance to go overseas and get away.
<v Ira Glass>鉴于——我希望你别误会——鉴于你的家庭限制这么多，我能理解你为什么想抓住机会去海外逃离。

00:50:49.320 --> 00:51:10.140
<v Joel>Yeah, I mean, some of the rules I can understand why. I accept it because it's a good idea. But it is getting kind of restrictive now that I'm getting older.
<v Joel>是的，我的意思是，有些规则我能理解为什么。我接受它们，因为它们是个好主意。但随着年龄增长，现在确实变得有点限制性了。

00:51:10.140 --> 00:51:36.770
<v Ira Glass>Act Seven, Made to Stumble Because of Me. All week I kept running into those guys who wanted to be on the radio, Matt and Eric. And on the last morning in West Virginia, I see Eric out by his car. The night before, he had driven some kids from his VBS back home after dinner. Eric's group was running this huge vacation Bible school at a place called Windmill Park in a tough section of Fairmont, West Virginia. Eric had gotten close to a lot of the kids.
<v Ira Glass>第七幕，因为我而绊倒。整个星期我都在遇到那些想上电台的家伙，马特和埃里克。在西弗吉尼亚州的最后一个早上，我看到埃里克在他车旁。前一天晚上，他晚饭后开车送一些参加VBS的孩子回家。埃里克的团队在西弗吉尼亚州费尔蒙特一个艰难的区域进行着大型度假圣经学校。埃里克和很多孩子变得很亲近。

00:51:36.770 --> 00:52:37.480
<v Eric>Yeah, it was really hard last night, taking them home. Some car was coming too close and they're like, it's a drive-by! And they're ducking under and they were scared. One of the kids left his bike at the park and he was all afraid that it was stolen. And stuff like that. And while we were pulling in there, they were like, keep your head low because there's bullets around here, and stuff like that. It's just really hard to think of these little first through fourth graders having to deal with this stuff every day. And we gave out prizes on our last day and a kid had to run home so that he could get the prizes to his house without having them taken from him. So it's rough. I mean, that's why I think, I'm almost-- I was almost like kind of down on the whole idea of VBS, because it's like you invest in these kids for a week, and they get close, and relationships are built. And then it's cut off. So I just felt like emotionally, it was just too hard on them. And last night I was really struggling with that whole idea.
<v Eric>是的，昨晚送他们回家真的很艰难。有辆车靠得太近，他们就说，是拍车门啊！他们躲在下面，他们很害怕。其中一个孩子把自行车留在了公园，他很担心被偷。之类的事。当我们开进去的时候，他们说，把头压低，因为这附近有子弹什么的。想到这些一年级到四年级的小孩子每天都要处理这种事，真是太难了。我们在最后一天发奖品，一个孩子必须跑回家，这样他才能在奖品被抢走之前把它们带回家。所以这很粗暴。我的意思是，这就是为什么我认为，我几乎——我对整个VBS的想法感到有点失望，因为这就像你在这一周里投资这些孩子，他们变得亲近，建立了关系。然后它就被切断了。所以我只是觉得在情感上，这对他们来说太沉重了。昨晚我真的在纠结整个想法。

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:43.700
<v Ira Glass>That's interesting. Earlier in the week, we were talking about if you guys were going to change, and in the end, you did see something you hadn't seen before.
<v Ira Glass>那很有趣。在一周早些时候，我们谈论过你们是否会改变，最后，你们确实看到了之前没看到的东西。

00:52:43.700 --> 00:53:03.730
<v Eric>Definitely. I mean, definitely. Because that was really hard. I was very broken up by the thought of whether it's right to do these one-week VBSs. And invest in this kids just to then leave them.
<v Eric>当然。我的意思是，当然。因为那真的很难。一想到做这些为期一周的VBS是否正确，我就感到很受伤。投资这些孩子只是为了然后离开他们。

00:53:03.730 --> 00:53:28.340
<v Ira Glass>At the end of their week, the Chicagoans are feeling proud of how far they've come-- with the vacation Bible school, with Angie and Paula and Crystal, who they invited to dinner out at the campsite on the last night. People promise to write to each other. When we ask Crystal which parts of the Bible did she think that she would be reading, now that Carol had pointed out some sections to her, she can't remember which sections Carol had said. She says she doesn't really read anything on her own.
<v Ira Glass>在一周结束时，芝加哥队为他们取得的进步感到自豪——度假圣经学校，还有安吉、宝拉和克里斯托，她们被邀请在最后一晚去营地吃饭。大家承诺互相写信。当我们问克里斯托，既然卡罗尔指出了其中的一些章节，她认为她会读圣经的哪些部分时，她记不起卡罗尔说的是哪些部分了。她说她自己其实什么都不读。

00:53:28.340 --> 00:53:39.300
<v Ira Glass>At services that last night, some kids from the East Coast get up to perform a song. They're in a band called Iris and the song is about one of the unique burdens of being a teenager and a Christian.
<v Ira Glass>在最后一晚的礼拜上，一些东海岸的孩子上台表演了一首歌。他们在一个名为“Iris”的乐队里，这首歌是关于作为青少年和基督徒的独特负担之一。

00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:54.030
<v Boy>So, hopefully, I'd like to encourage you all to not let it stop here, but when you go back, if you have friends or family that you can witness to, continue to share the love of Christ with people.
<v Boy>所以，我希望能鼓励你们所有人不要让它止步于此，当你们回去时，如果你有可以作见证的朋友或家人，继续与人们分享基督的爱。

00:53:54.030 --> 00:55:06.280
<v Girl>[SINGING] Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah. Are you so self-assured in everything you confide? Are my beliefs so absurd? I would not try to hide. I'm not trying to push you, but I hate to see you like this. I love you too much to let you die in ignorance. You're on a ledge and the ground beneath is breaking. You try to swim, you're sinking slow but surely grab his hands. Hey, friend, I won't have your blood upon my hands. Hey friend, what can I do to make you understand? To make you understand?
<v Girl>[演唱] 是的，是的，是的，是的。是的，是的，是的，是的。你在你所信任的一切中都如此自信吗？我的信仰如此荒谬吗？我不会试图隐藏。我不是要逼你，但我讨厌看到你这样。我爱你太深，不能让你死于无知。你站在悬崖边，下面的地正在破碎。你试着游泳，你正在慢慢下沉但肯定会抓住他的手。嘿，朋友，我不会让你的血沾在我的手上。嘿朋友，我能做什么让你明白？让你明白？

00:55:06.280 --> 00:55:27.040
<v Ira Glass>As for whether the Chicago kids are up to the task of actually proselytizing their non-Christian friends, they do not seem eager or ready. It's one thing to befriend strangers in another state. It's another kind of commitment to their faith to try it at home. It's a sacrifice. But on the long drive home, Joel, the future missionary, says he doesn't think it'll happen with the group.
<v Ira Glass>至于芝加哥的孩子们是否有能力真正去向非基督徒朋友传教，他们似乎并不渴望或准备好。与外州的陌生人交友是一回事，尝试在家里传教是另一种对信仰的承诺。这是一种牺牲。但在回家的漫长车程中，未来的宣教士乔尔说，他不认为这会在这个小组中发生。

00:55:27.040 --> 00:56:03.390
<v Joel>Either the people won't want to come, or at least as far as I'm concerned, I'm too nervous to ask them. And I already-- and I also-- in the case of most of my non-Christian friends, I already know what their answer would be. They don't-- they're fine with me being Christian. But they don't have any interest in it themselves, at least not at this point.
<v Joel>要么人们不想来，或者就我而言，我太紧张不敢问他们。而且我已经——而且我还——就我大多数非基督徒朋友而言，我已经知道他们的答案会是什么了。他们不介意我是基督徒。但他们自己没有任何兴趣，至少目前还没有。

00:56:03.390 --> 00:56:59.450
<v Ira Glass>In the four months since the trip, only one member of the group has brought a non-Christian friend to youth group. As for contact with Crystal and Paula and Angie, an adult leader wrote a letter to Angie, and sent a Bible that they all signed. There hasn't been a lot more. And maybe this lack of proselytizing energy is not such a bad thing. If Dan is going to stick to his goal from the trip, helping the kids examine what they believe, the real tests of faith for most people are not usually in interactions with people hundreds of miles away, or with people they're trying to convert. Real tests of faith happen with the people closest to you, with parents and friends. It was a bunch of Christians who made Arden question her faith. It's a desire to kiss a nice Christian girl that made Joel question his. Those questions are likely to continue.
<v Ira Glass>自那次旅行以来的四个月里，小组中只有一名成员带过非基督徒朋友参加青年团契。至于与克里斯托、宝拉和安吉的联系，一位成年领袖给安吉写了一封信，并寄去了一本他们都签名的圣经。并没有更多的联系。也许这种缺乏传教活力的事情并不是一件坏事。如果丹要坚持他旅行的目标，帮助孩子们审视他们所相信的东西，那么大多数人信仰的真正考验通常不在于与数百英里外的人互动，也不在于与他们试图改造的人互动。信仰的真正考验发生在与你最亲近的人身上，与父母和朋友。正是这群基督徒让阿登质疑她的信仰。是想要亲吻一个可爱的基督徒女孩让乔尔质疑他的。这些问题很可能会持续下去。

00:56:59.450 --> 00:57:10.130
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced and reported today by Susan Burton and myself, with help from Alex Blumberg, Nancy Updike, Blue Chevigny, Starlee Kine, and Todd Bachmann. Julie Snyder was our editor on the story.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我们的节目今天由苏珊·伯顿和我自己制作和报道，亚历克斯·布隆伯格、南希·厄普代克、布鲁·切维尼、斯塔莉·凯恩和托德·巴赫曼提供了帮助。朱莉·斯奈德是我们故事的编辑。

